A notification that goes wakes every thread waiting on it before it asks
whether an interrupt is pending, and asks only once the queue is empty,
so a destroy takes as long as there are waiters before the tick is heard.
It wakes the same threads with the same status, so only the counting harness catches it.

Caught-by: fuzz-work
Report: the paid loop at kernel/sched.c took a second step without asking intr_pending

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -320,7 +320,7 @@ bool sched_forget(struct obj_header *o, bool preempt)
             t->frame.regs[REG_A0] = KERR_INVALID_CAP;
             t->frame.regs[REG_A1] = 0;
             sched_ready(t);
-            if (ntfn->waiters != 0 && cap_stop_here(preempt)) {
+            if (ntfn->waiters == 0 && cap_stop_here(preempt)) {
                 return false;
             }
         }
